# 3. Initial Marker Placement

Markers are initially placed using drag and drop.

Workflow:

1. Open Scene Markings.
2. Expand:
   Intro → Part B — Wake Up
3. Drag a marker such as PLAYER START from the editor.
4. Drop it directly onto the game world.
5. Store that world position for the marker.
6. Display the marker at that position in editor mode.

Do NOT automatically choose positions for these markers.

I will manually place all three markers.


# 4. Marker Appearance

Placed scene markers should be visually distinct from normal world assets.

Use a clearly visible RED CIRCULAR MARKER.

Display its name inside or immediately beside it, for example:

[ PLAYER START ]

[ MITCHY START ]

[ MITCHY EXIT ]

They should remain readable over the ASCII world.

These are development/editor overlays and should not visually resemble actual game objects.


# 5. Reposition Existing Markers

After a marker has been placed, allow it to be repositioned directly in the world using:

ALT + LEFT CLICK + DRAG

Behavior:

- Hold Alt.
- Left-click an existing marker.
- Keep the mouse button held.
- Drag it to another world position.
- Release to save its new coordinates.

Normal left-click behavior must remain unchanged.

A marker should therefore NOT move from an ordinary click or drag.

In short:

Drag & Drop from editor → initial placement

Alt + Left Click + Drag → reposition an already placed marker


# 6. Marker Persistence

Marker positions must persist so that refreshing/restarting the development session does not require me to place them again.

Store markers separately from normal world assets.

Each marker should have a stable semantic ID rather than relying on its visible label or coordinates.

For example:

intro.partB.playerStart
intro.partB.mitchyStart
intro.partB.mitchyExit

Later cinematic logic should be able to request a marker position using this stable ID.

Design this so additional marker IDs can easily be registered for future sequences.


# 7. Editor-Only Visibility

Scene markers are metadata, NOT game entities.

They must:

- Be visible while the relevant development/editor mode is active.
- Be selectable/repositionable while editing.
- Never participate in collision.
- Never affect gameplay.
- Never be collectible or interactable as game objects.
- Never appear during normal gameplay.
- Never appear during the actual cinematic.
- Never appear in production builds.

When the editor is closed, the red marker visualization must disappear.

Their stored coordinates must still remain available to development/cinematic logic.


